      "_evidence": "The canonical correlation analyses were conducted to determine the strength of association should a signiﬁcant relationship exist between sets of variables, the amount of variance explained in each set of variables by the other set of variables, as well as which, and in what direction (positive or negative), individual variables contributed to the multivariate relationship. For females, a statistically signiﬁcant relationship was found between the set of self-conceptions and the set of muscle dysmorphia symptoms, χ2 (14) = 83.17, p <.0001. The canonical correlation of Rc = .58 suggested a moderately strong relationship between self-esteem as well as perceived body attractiveness and the set of muscle dysmorphia symptoms. Self-conceptions explained only 7% of the variance in the set of muscle dysmorphia symptoms, while the set of muscle dysmorphia symptoms explained 26% of the variance in self-conceptions. Moreover, canonical loadings, where a loading of .30 or above is typically used as a cutoff for interpreting the contribution of individual variables (Tabachnick & Fidell, 2001), suggested both self-esteem and perceived body attractiveness contributed to the multivariate relationship. The muscle dysmorphia symptoms that contributed to the relationship included general muscle dysmorphia and physique protection along with, to a lesser degree, pharmacological use and dietary behavior. The canonical loadings appear in Table 2 and indicate (by the sets of positive versus negative loadings) that self-conceptions were inversely related to the muscle dysmorphia symptoms. In addition, for females, the set of negative affect variables was signiﬁcantly related to the set of muscle dysmorphia symptoms, χ2 (14) = 114.00, p <.0001. The canonical correlation between the two sets of variables was Rc = .66. Negative affect variables explained 9% of the variance in the muscle dysmorphia symptoms, while the set of muscle dysmorphia symptoms explained 32% of the variance in depression and social physique anxiety. Canonical loadings indicated that social physique anxiety and, to a lesser degree, depression contributed to the multivariate relationship. Also contributing to the multivariate relationship were the muscle dysmorphia symptoms of physique protection and general muscle dysmorphia. The negative affect variables were positively related to the muscle dysmorphia symptoms in that higher levels of physique protection and general muscle dysmorphia were associated with more social physique anxiety and depression.",
